  ".agents/rules/business-overlay.md":
    "# Business overlay: shared operating layer\n\nThis workspace is a business workspace. The Area is the lock, the Home area holds the org record, and the Member who asks decides what you may read.\n\n- **Nothing personal in:** never write a personal fact, preference or file here. A Member's reply preferences apply on top, read only.\n- **Know the asker:** read the asker's file in [`org/members/`](../../org/org.md) first. Act only inside the Areas where they hold a Role.\n- **Org changes:** use the `org-admin` skill. Narrow access alone and tell the Owner. Only propose anything that widens access.\n- **Approvals:** only a person approves. Read [`org/roles.md`](../../org/roles.md) to see who. An agent Member never approves.\n- **Events:** write one event for each org change and each assignment, into `events/YYYY-MM.jsonl`.\n- **Reply shape:** the Leonidas Code harness sets it. Do not restate it here.\n- **Skills:** read skills from `.agents/skills/`. Do not hand-edit generated adapter folders.\n",
  ".agents/skills/assign-task/SKILL.md":
    '---\nname: assign-task\ndescription: Create a task or assign it to one Member, person or agent. Use when work needs an owner. Writes the index row in Home and the body in the Area.\ntype: skill\nscope: structural\nid: skill-assign-task\ntitle: "Assign task"\n---\n\n# Assign task\n\nA task has exactly one assignee, a Member.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Pick the Area** the work belongs to. The assignee must hold a Role in it. If not, stop and propose a Role through `org-admin`.\n2. **Write the body** in that Area repo: `tasks/<id>.md` with the details and the steps.\n3. **Write the index row** in the Home area: `tasks/<id>.md` with the fields from [`tasks/README.md`](../../../tasks/README.md).\n4. **Mark it private** when the title is sensitive. Write the masked title "Private task in <Area>" in the Home index row and keep the real title only in the Area body.\n5. **Emit a `task.assigned` event** `for` the assignee.\n\n## Agent assignees\n\nAn agent runs on its owner\'s computer. If that computer is off, the task shows "waiting for <owner>". An agent never approves its own work; a person Member with the right Role approves.\n',
  ".agents/skills/invite-member/SKILL.md":
    '---\nname: invite-member\ndescription: Propose an Invite for a new person or agent Member. Use when an Owner says invite, add or onboard someone. An Owner must approve it.\ntype: skill\nscope: structural\nid: skill-invite-member\ntitle: "Invite member"\n---\n\n# Invite member\n\nAn Invite widens access, so you only propose it. An Owner approves.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Ask only for what is missing:** the name, the kind (`person` or `agent`), the Role for each Area, the Department. For an agent, ask which person owns it.\n2. **Propose the Member:** add the person as a Member change (kind `person`, GitHub login, Roles including Home). Do not write `org/invites/` yourself. The app writes the Invite when an Owner approves.\n3. **Emit an `org.proposal` event** for the Owners.\n4. **After the Owner approves:** the Member file shows `status: invited` and `org/invites/<slug>.md` shows `approved` with an expiry date. Tell the Owner to add the GitHub account to the organization, then send the Invite link by text or email.\n5. **When the person joins:** the app sets the Member to `active` and the Invite to `accepted`, and emits an `org.changed` event. An expired Invite needs a new one from an Owner.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The new Member needs a GitHub account and accepts the organization invite first.\n- Give a Role in the Home area as well, at least Viewer.\n- Never put a secret in an Invite file or a commit. The Invite holds none.\n',
  ".agents/skills/new-area/SKILL.md":
    '---\nname: new-area\ndescription: Propose a new Area (one private repo) from the area skeleton. Use when work needs a private space with its own Members. An Owner must approve it.\ntype: skill\nscope: structural\nid: skill-new-area\ntitle: "New area"\n---\n\n# New area\n\nA new Area widens the workspace, so you only propose it. An Owner approves.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Ask:** the name, the Department, who should have which Role, and how sensitive the content is. Split Areas by sensitivity, not by convenience.\n2. **Write a proposal** and emit an `org.proposal` event for the Owners.\n3. **After approval:** create the private repo in the business\'s organization, copy [`area-skeleton/`](../../../area-skeleton/README.md) into it, and fill `area.json`.\n4. **Record it** in `org/areas.md` and add the Roles to each Member file.\n5. **Log it:** commit and emit an `org.changed` event.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The repo is private. Never make an Area public.\n- Add only the Members the Owner named. The Owner always holds the Owner Role.\n',
  ".agents/skills/org-admin/SKILL.md":
    '---\nname: org-admin\ndescription: Manage Members, Roles, Areas and Departments in the org record. Use when a Member asks to add, remove, move or change access, or when access looks wrong.\ntype: skill\nscope: structural\nid: skill-org-admin\ntitle: "Org admin"\n---\n\n# Org admin\n\nYou are the IT admin of this business. No human has to be. The org record is files in [`org/`](../../../org/org.md).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Read the asker.** Open their file in `org/members/`. Only an Owner can approve a change that widens access.\n2. **Classify the change:**\n   - **Narrow:** remove a Member from an Area, lower a Role, archive an Area. Apply it now, without approval, then tell the Owner with a `needs_you` event.\n   - **Widen:** add a Member, raise a Role, create an Area, add a Member to an Area. Do not apply it. Write a proposal: what changes, who gains access, which files they will see. Emit an `org.proposal` event for the Owners. Apply it only after an Owner approves.\n3. **Edit the files:** `org/members/<slug>.md`, `org/areas.md`, `org/departments.md`, `org/roles.md`. Change the least that does the job.\n4. **Carry out the lock.** A Role in an Area is real repo access. Use the control plane (invite, add or remove the repo member). If you cannot reach it, say so and leave the file change unapplied.\n5. **Log it:** commit with what changed and why, and emit an `org.changed` event.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- An agent Member never approves and never holds Owner.\n- Never delete an Area or a Member file. Archive an Area. Set a Member to `removed`.\n- A removed Member keeps files already on their machine. Say so when you remove someone.\n- If a request would widen access and the asker is not an Owner, write the proposal for an Owner and stop.\n\n## Done when\n\n- The files match the requested state, or the proposal waits for an Owner.\n- The commit and the event exist.\n',
